WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.510 --> 00:00:07.010
BABATUNDE OGUNLADE:
Hello, everyone.

00:00:07.010 --> 00:00:08.718
Today we'll be working
through Goodie Bag

00:00:08.718 --> 00:00:10.552
3, which is on ionic solids.

00:00:10.552 --> 00:00:12.260
In order to work
through this Goodie Bag,

00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:15.080
you'll need a conductivity
meter, some small measuring

00:00:15.080 --> 00:00:19.310
cups, some medium sized cups, a
small scale, a writing utensil,

00:00:19.310 --> 00:00:21.800
some nitrile gloves,
some stirrers,

00:00:21.800 --> 00:00:23.240
and four unique solids.

00:00:23.240 --> 00:00:26.480
Today, I'll be using sodium
chloride, magnesium sulfate,

00:00:26.480 --> 00:00:29.240
magnesium oxide, and sucrose.

00:00:29.240 --> 00:00:32.540
Our main objective today is to
use solubility and conductivity

00:00:32.540 --> 00:00:34.580
measurements to determine
if a given solid is

00:00:34.580 --> 00:00:36.850
an ionic or covalent compound.

00:00:36.850 --> 00:00:39.720
And as we do this, I'd like you
to think about two questions.

00:00:39.720 --> 00:00:42.530
First, how do solids
dissolve in water?

00:00:42.530 --> 00:00:45.590
And second, what factors
may influence how conductive

00:00:45.590 --> 00:00:47.920
a given solid is in water?

00:00:47.920 --> 00:00:50.420
So first we're going to look
at the solubility of our solids

00:00:50.420 --> 00:00:51.680
in water.

00:00:51.680 --> 00:00:54.590
And to do this, we're going to
dissolve 1 gram of each solid

00:00:54.590 --> 00:00:56.660
in 30 milliliters of water.

00:00:56.660 --> 00:00:59.040
I've already weighed
out my solids here.

00:00:59.040 --> 00:01:01.610
And I've also already
weighed out my water.

00:01:01.610 --> 00:01:04.012
Notice how I'm wearing a
pair of nitrile gloves.

00:01:04.012 --> 00:01:05.720
The compounds that
I'm working with today

00:01:05.720 --> 00:01:08.210
aren't that dangerous, but
just as general safety,

00:01:08.210 --> 00:01:11.160
it's good to wear gloves
when handling compounds.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:13.970
So I'm going to pour each
solute into each respective cup

00:01:13.970 --> 00:01:17.330
of water and stir and mix for
about a minute to two minutes

00:01:17.330 --> 00:01:21.120
and see if the
compound dissolves.

00:01:21.120 --> 00:01:23.760
So after one to two minutes
of stirring and dissolving,

00:01:23.760 --> 00:01:26.910
you can see that our magnesium
oxide right here has not

00:01:26.910 --> 00:01:28.150
dissolved in our water.

00:01:28.150 --> 00:01:30.548
You can see a very clear
boundary between the solute,

00:01:30.548 --> 00:01:32.340
which has settled at
the bottom of the cup,

00:01:32.340 --> 00:01:34.320
and the water, which is on top.

00:01:34.320 --> 00:01:36.510
If you compare that to
our sodium chloride,

00:01:36.510 --> 00:01:38.320
though, if you look
very carefully,

00:01:38.320 --> 00:01:40.860
you can see that our
solute has dissolved

00:01:40.860 --> 00:01:43.302
very well in our water.

00:01:43.302 --> 00:01:44.760
So the next thing
we'll be doing is

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:47.220
measuring the conductivity
of our compounds

00:01:47.220 --> 00:01:48.875
when dissolved in water.

00:01:48.875 --> 00:01:50.250
And to do that,
we're going to be

00:01:50.250 --> 00:01:54.010
using a handy dandy conductivity
meter that I have right here.

00:01:54.010 --> 00:01:56.820
So I have some cups
of water prepared here

00:01:56.820 --> 00:01:59.880
to be used as both a
baseline correction

00:01:59.880 --> 00:02:02.580
when we're measuring our
conductivity of our compounds

00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:06.750
and as a water bath to clean our
probe in between measurements.

00:02:06.750 --> 00:02:09.600
OK, so first I'm going to turn
on my conductivity measure.

00:02:12.447 --> 00:02:14.280
And I'm going to make
sure that my units are

00:02:14.280 --> 00:02:16.320
set to microsiemens
per centimeter,

00:02:16.320 --> 00:02:18.580
or some equivalent unit.

00:02:18.580 --> 00:02:21.650
And first I'm going to measure
a baseline of just regular water

00:02:21.650 --> 00:02:22.150
here.

00:02:22.150 --> 00:02:24.030
I have tap water here.

00:02:24.030 --> 00:02:28.290
And so when I do
that, I see that I'm

00:02:28.290 --> 00:02:31.000
measuring around 900
microsiemens per centimeter.

00:02:31.000 --> 00:02:33.355
So that is the connectivity
of my water as is.

00:02:33.355 --> 00:02:34.980
And I'm going to use
that as a baseline

00:02:34.980 --> 00:02:36.438
when I'm measuring
the connectivity

00:02:36.438 --> 00:02:37.800
of my other compounds.

00:02:40.650 --> 00:02:42.530
So since this is water,
it's already clean,

00:02:42.530 --> 00:02:44.300
and now I'm going to measure
my sodium chloride, which

00:02:44.300 --> 00:02:44.883
is right here.

00:02:50.960 --> 00:02:55.080
And so right now I'm reading
around 4,700 microsiemens

00:02:55.080 --> 00:02:56.758
per centimeter for
my sodium chloride.

00:02:56.758 --> 00:02:58.050
I'm going to take note of that.

00:02:58.050 --> 00:02:59.883
And eventually, when
I'm collecting my data,

00:02:59.883 --> 00:03:04.230
I'm going to subtract my water
baseline from this value here.

00:03:04.230 --> 00:03:05.970
Make sure in between
each measurement

00:03:05.970 --> 00:03:09.240
that you dip the probe in
the water bath to clean it,

00:03:09.240 --> 00:03:13.210
and you can continue on with
the rest of your measurements.

00:03:13.210 --> 00:03:15.180
So the last experiment
we're going to do today

00:03:15.180 --> 00:03:17.460
is to measure the
solubility of our compounds

00:03:17.460 --> 00:03:21.270
in water, except with 2
grams instead of 1 gram.

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:23.820
So I've measured out an
additional 1 gram of each solid

00:03:23.820 --> 00:03:25.590
here, and I'm just
going to pour each one

00:03:25.590 --> 00:03:27.875
into each respective
cup, mix and stir.

00:03:27.875 --> 00:03:29.250
And after one to
two minutes, I'm

00:03:29.250 --> 00:03:32.750
going to see if each
compound is dissolved.

00:03:32.750 --> 00:03:35.030
So again, we have
our magnesium oxide

00:03:35.030 --> 00:03:37.060
after round two of solubility.

00:03:37.060 --> 00:03:39.380
Again, we can see that
our solute has not

00:03:39.380 --> 00:03:40.190
dissolved in water.

00:03:40.190 --> 00:03:43.100
There's a very clear boundary
between the water on top

00:03:43.100 --> 00:03:44.640
and the solute on the bottom.

00:03:44.640 --> 00:03:47.120
So in this case, round
one solubility and round

00:03:47.120 --> 00:03:49.340
two solubility look
identical, but it may not

00:03:49.340 --> 00:03:52.160
be the case for our
other three solutes.

00:03:52.160 --> 00:03:54.650
All right, so now that we've
finished our last solubility

00:03:54.650 --> 00:03:56.960
test, let's put all our data--

00:03:56.960 --> 00:03:59.840
our first solubility test,
our connectivity test,

00:03:59.840 --> 00:04:01.370
and our second solubility test--

00:04:01.370 --> 00:04:03.320
on the board.

00:04:03.320 --> 00:04:07.230
So here we have a chart of all
the data from our experiments.

00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:10.140
I have the compounds that we
use going down right here.

00:04:10.140 --> 00:04:13.470
And I have solubility here
at 1 gram and 2 grams,

00:04:13.470 --> 00:04:16.660
and conductivity after
water subtraction here.

00:04:16.660 --> 00:04:19.440
So before going into
this data, first thing

00:04:19.440 --> 00:04:22.019
I would like us to notice
is that for sodium chloride

00:04:22.019 --> 00:04:25.890
and for magnesium sulfate,
these conductivity values

00:04:25.890 --> 00:04:27.990
are much lower than I expected.

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:30.230
This should be close to
the 10,000 microsiemens,

00:04:30.230 --> 00:04:32.430
and this should be closer
to 8,000 microsiemens

00:04:32.430 --> 00:04:34.260
per centimeter, but
the fact that we're

00:04:34.260 --> 00:04:37.380
able to measure conductivity
for these respective compounds

00:04:37.380 --> 00:04:40.570
gives us insight into what
type of compounds they are.

00:04:40.570 --> 00:04:42.640
So first, if we look
at sodium chloride,

00:04:42.640 --> 00:04:46.320
we could see that at
both 1 gram and 2 grams,

00:04:46.320 --> 00:04:48.720
our sodium chloride was
able to dissolve in water.

00:04:48.720 --> 00:04:52.020
And we were able to measure
conductivity readings for this.

00:04:52.020 --> 00:04:53.970
This means our
sodium chloride was

00:04:53.970 --> 00:04:57.420
able to dissociate into ions
when dissolved in water.

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.040
And this is an
extra check for us

00:04:59.040 --> 00:05:01.125
to determine that sodium
chloride is indeed

00:05:01.125 --> 00:05:02.085
an ionic compound.

00:05:06.030 --> 00:05:08.370
Next we can look
at magnesium oxide.

00:05:08.370 --> 00:05:13.770
And we see that for magnesium
oxide, at 1 gram and 2 grams,

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.550
we weren't able to dissolve
the magnesium oxide in water.

00:05:16.550 --> 00:05:18.570
And not only that,
because of that,

00:05:18.570 --> 00:05:20.490
we weren't able to
measure any conductivity.

00:05:20.490 --> 00:05:24.600
But we know that magnesium
oxide is an ionic compound

00:05:24.600 --> 00:05:26.770
just from our
understanding of bonding.

00:05:26.770 --> 00:05:30.670
So we had to think more closely
about why this is the case.

00:05:30.670 --> 00:05:33.630
So magnesium oxide
and sodium chloride

00:05:33.630 --> 00:05:36.480
are both ionic compounds,
but magnesium oxide

00:05:36.480 --> 00:05:39.810
has a much higher lattice
energy than sodium chloride.

00:05:39.810 --> 00:05:43.800
This means that when magnesium
oxide is placed in water,

00:05:43.800 --> 00:05:45.780
it's more energetically
favorable for it

00:05:45.780 --> 00:05:50.070
to stay as a solid than it
is to dissociate into ions

00:05:50.070 --> 00:05:56.100
of magnesium and oxygen.
So magnesium oxide is still

00:05:56.100 --> 00:05:58.380
an ionic compound, but
it's lattice energy

00:05:58.380 --> 00:06:01.410
is high enough such that it does
not dissolve and does not give

00:06:01.410 --> 00:06:04.470
us conductivity measurements.

00:06:04.470 --> 00:06:06.630
So it's still ionic.

00:06:06.630 --> 00:06:08.700
Now we can look at
magnesium sulfate.

00:06:08.700 --> 00:06:12.530
And we see that for 1 gram,
we do get some solubility.

00:06:12.530 --> 00:06:15.635
And at 2 grams, we
get no solubility.

00:06:15.635 --> 00:06:17.700
It was not able to dissolve.

00:06:17.700 --> 00:06:20.220
And we're able to measure
some conductivity.

00:06:20.220 --> 00:06:23.620
This means that magnesium
sulfate dissociated

00:06:23.620 --> 00:06:26.460
into respective ions and
we're able to measure

00:06:26.460 --> 00:06:28.650
some conductivity
while in water.

00:06:28.650 --> 00:06:31.410
But magnesium sulfate
has a limited solubility

00:06:31.410 --> 00:06:33.397
in water, which
is why at 2 grams

00:06:33.397 --> 00:06:34.980
it wasn't able to
dissolve completely.

00:06:34.980 --> 00:06:40.020
So magnesium sulfate is
still an ionic compound.

00:06:40.020 --> 00:06:42.090
Finally, if we
look at sucrose, we

00:06:42.090 --> 00:06:44.580
see that we were able
to get 1 gram of sucrose

00:06:44.580 --> 00:06:47.010
to dissolve in water,
and 2 grams of sucrose

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.540
was able to dissolve in water.

00:06:48.540 --> 00:06:51.570
But we were not able to
get any conductivity.

00:06:51.570 --> 00:06:54.090
This means that sucrose
dissolved in water,

00:06:54.090 --> 00:06:57.750
but did not dissociate
into respective ions.

00:06:57.750 --> 00:06:59.790
So if you think about
the structure of sucrose,

00:06:59.790 --> 00:07:05.790
sucrose has a bunch of polar
groups on it, which make

00:07:05.790 --> 00:07:07.530
it able to dissolve in water.

00:07:07.530 --> 00:07:10.570
But sucrose does not
dissociate into ions,

00:07:10.570 --> 00:07:13.050
which is why we were not able
to measure any conductivity.

00:07:13.050 --> 00:07:15.325
So sucrose is a
covalent compound.

00:07:20.560 --> 00:07:23.490
So by using our knowledge of
bonding and our solubility

00:07:23.490 --> 00:07:26.550
and conductivity data,
we're able to affirm

00:07:26.550 --> 00:07:29.190
the identity of our compounds.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:32.490
That is, whether or not
they're ionic or covalent.

00:07:32.490 --> 00:07:34.080
So today we looked
at how the type

00:07:34.080 --> 00:07:36.300
and strength of
bonding of a solid

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:38.280
influences its properties.

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:41.100
Specifically, we looked at
how these aspects of bonding

00:07:41.100 --> 00:07:44.100
influence these solubility
and conductivity

00:07:44.100 --> 00:07:46.670
of these solids in water.

